{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789306197,"nonce":"JbtEtefkGtc0p/HJUKZw+URQvp8=","title":"Host natives can carry a context word every tier passes first","message":"A host that stands one C function behind many natives (caribou: every\nmember of a class another language published) needs the native to be\ntold which member it is. Without that the host generates one C entry per\nnative, which is a table of trampolines for every (arity, slot) pair.\n\nA host native may now register a context word (native_lib::HostNative,\nHostMethod::context). When it is non-zero the interpreter, the Cranelift\ntier (both the opcode and the AIR lowering) and the LLVM tier's caller\nthunk pass it as the leading argument, ahead of the arguments the\nprogram's signature declares. Ahead-of-time linking refuses such a native\nfor now: the word is a run-time address. The host_module end-to-end test\nbinds its bump native with a context in every mode, interp, hybrid,\ncranelift alone, llvm alone and jit.","files":null}]}